    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 051144\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n445 AM PDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Breezy conditions persist today with a slight (5 to 15 percent) \n  chance of isolated showers and thunderstorms in our southern zones.\n\n* Shower and thunderstorm chances increase and spread across the \n  Mojave Desert on Sunday as moisture surges northward.\n\n* Drier conditions expected for Labor Day, though monsoonal\n  moisture and warmer temperatures return midweek.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Friday.\n\nGenerally expecting a quiet and dry day across the region today. \nWill need to watch if showers and thunderstorms are able to develop \nalong a moisture boundary as it surges northward this afternoon, \nparticularly in southern Mohave County and the Colorado River Valley \nsouth of Lake Mohave. Due to the dry low-levels, these showers and \nthunderstorms may simply amount to virga with gusty winds from their \ndirection. Breezy south to southwest winds are expected again this \nafternoon, with magnitudes slightly lower than yesterday's winds \nwith gust speeds between 20 and 30 MPH in many locations. \n\nThe main concern in the forecast is Sunday as moisture is advected \nup from the tropics, resulting in a plume of precipitation across \nthe region. PWATs climb over 1.00 inches by Sunday. Models are all \nin decent agreement that this stripe of precipitation will develop \nsomewhere across the region, but exactly where is still in question. \nThe best guess is a band of stratiform rain setting up through the \nwestern Mojave Desert through Clark County into southwestern Utah on \nSunday as this is where the signal for measurable rainfall is \nhighest. Areas south and north of this are where the uncertainty \nexists, especially Death Valley, Pahrump, and Caliente to the north, \nthen Kingman and Bullhead City to the south. The other unknown for \nthis event is how much convection will be able to develop alongside \nor within the rain band, but current instability fields are not \nstrongly supportive of thunderstorm development. The highest chance \nfor rainfall impacts will be the western Mojave Desert through the \nSpring Mountains where there is about a 50% chance for over 0.50 \ninches of total rainfall Sunday and Sunday night (with a bulk of the \nrainfall occurring before Sunday evening). Las Vegas may struggle to \nsee much rainfall due to rainshadowing from the nearby terrain, but \nrain should fall at some point on Sunday in the valley. Roadway \nponding, minor flooding, and responses in typical flashy areas will \nbe possible where the heaviest rainfall does fall. \n\nThings shut down Sunday night as the trough swings inland. On \nMonday, PWATs dry out quickly as westerly anti-cyclonic flows sets \nup aloft. After a cool down on Sunday with the rain and falling \nheights, it will start warming back up on Monday as the trough lifts \nout. After a down day on Monday, will need to monitor how moisture \nmoves back in from both the tropics in the Pacific as well as \nmonsoonal moisture as the high repositions itself somewhere over the \nSouthwest US. Temperatures will depend on how much moisture returns \nto the region, but should remain at least near normal for this time \nof the year.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...South-\nsouthwest winds with gusts between 20 and 25 kts expected this \nafternoon. Expect gradually increasing cloud cover after sunset \ntoday. Periods of showers and a slight chance of thunderstorms \npossible after 10Z Sunday with CIGs dropping to 8 to 10 kft. Light \nshowers expected after 15Z Sunday. Temperatures will remain below \n100F through at least Tuesday.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Gusty south-\nsouthwesterly winds expected this afternoon with gust speeds between \n20 and 30 kts. The exception will be KDAG gusting from the west. \nExpect gradually increasing cloud cover this afternoon through this \nevening. Colorado River Valley TAF sites, KDAG, and Las Vegas Valley \nTAF sites have a chance of shower and thunderstorm activity over the \nterminal starting between 08 and 10Z. With precipitation, expect \nCIGs dropping to 8 to 10 kft.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
